/* ══ Card appearance themes ════════════════════════════════════════════════

   Four treatments of the same card. A designer or an administrator picks one
   on the Dashboard card of Site Settings > Appearance and it is stored in
   dashboard.widget_theme; welcome.php writes the choice onto #content, so
   everything below is scoped to that attribute and the settings screen's own
   copy of the system status card is left out of it.

   "flat" has no rules here at all, and that is the point. Flat IS the block of
   per-widget backgrounds above, so the default costs nothing and an
   installation that never changes it keeps exactly the dashboard it had.
   The other three replace background, border and shadow, and they read
   --pg-accent -- which every widget already declares -- so one rule set dresses
   all twenty-two cards instead of three more colour tables.

   Specificity, because it decides whether this works at all: the per-widget
   dark rules above are html[data-bs-theme="dark"] .widget[widget-id="N"], which
   is (0,3,1). A theme rule written as [data-widget-theme] .card.widget is
   (0,3,0) and would lose to them -- the cards would take the theme in light and
   drop it in dark. Each block therefore repeats the html[...] prefix for its
   dark half rather than relying on source order. */

/* ── Strength ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   One dial per theme. Every colour in the three blocks below is mixed at one
   of these, so a theme that reads too loud is one number here rather than a
   dozen values spread across six rules.

   Neon runs at full strength and glass and aurora do not, and the reason is
   where each of them puts its colour rather than how much it uses. Neon keeps
   the hue on the border: twenty-three rims read as an outline. Glass and
   aurora lay it across the face of the card, under the list, and on a near
   black ground a saturated veil has nothing to be diluted by -- twenty-three
   of those at full strength is a colour riot, and dark is where it showed.

   Dimming alone was not the fix. What these two needed was for the colour to
   stop before the reading area starts, which is the geometry in each block,
   and for the dark tints to be mixed INTO the card background instead of
   floated over it as a translucent accent. A colour mixed with the ground is a
   tint of the card; the same colour at the same alpha over the ground is a
   film on top of it, and the second is what made the text look submerged.

   ── The selector, and why it is shaped like that ──
   Every rule below reads `[data-widget-theme] .pg-widget > .card.widget`. The
   `.pg-widget >` is not decoration and not there for readability: it is what
   lifts the selector from (0,3,0) to (0,4,0), which is the only thing putting
   it above the per-widget dark rules further up this file --
   `html[data-bs-theme="dark"] .widget[widget-id="N"]`, at (0,3,1).

   Written the short way a theme applies in light and is beaten by the flat
   dark tint the moment the panel goes dark. That is not hypothetical: aurora
   was rewritten so its light and dark halves held the same values, the
   now-identical dark half was deleted as dead code, and the theme silently
   stopped applying in dark. Every card fell back to its flat tint and the
   computed background-image read `none`. Raising the base selector means a
   dark twin exists only where the VALUES differ -- never to win an argument. */
[data-widget-theme="neon"] {
  --pg-fx-edge: 42%;   /* border */
  --pg-fx-lift: 100%;  /* the halo under the card */
}
[data-widget-theme="glass"] {
  --pg-fx-top: 14%;    /* accent at the head of the sheet, gone by mid-card */
  --pg-fx-edge: 24%;
}
[data-widget-theme="aurora"] {
  --pg-fx-one: 16%;    /* bloom at the top left */
  --pg-fx-two: 12%;    /* and at the bottom right */
  --pg-fx-edge: 20%;
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] [data-widget-theme="neon"] {
  --pg-fx-edge: 55%;
  --pg-fx-lift: 100%;
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] [data-widget-theme="glass"] {
  --pg-fx-top: 16%;
  --pg-fx-edge: 26%;
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] [data-widget-theme="aurora"] {
  --pg-fx-one: 22%;
  --pg-fx-two: 16%;
  --pg-fx-edge: 22%;
}

/* ── Neon ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The ground stays neutral and the light comes off the edge: a bleed just
   inside the border, and a low halo under the card.

   The bleed is a wide blur with a large negative spread, so however wide the
   blur, it only reaches a few pixels in from the border. That is what keeps it
   a rim of light rather than a lit card, and it is why this theme survives at
   full strength where the other two could not: nothing it does happens where
   the numbers are printed.

   Deliberately asymmetric between the two panel themes. A glow is light
   emitted against darkness, so in dark it is the loudest of the four; on a
   white ground there is nothing for it to emit against and it settles into a
   coloured shadow. Faking parity would mean flooding the light theme with
   colour, which is what the flat theme already does better. */
[data-widget-theme="neon"] .pg-widget > .card.widget {
  background: var(--bs-card-bg);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) var(--pg-fx-edge), transparent);
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 0 22px -14px var(--pg-accent, #64748b),
    0 8px 26px -20px color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) var(--pg-fx-lift), transparent);
}

/* ── Glass ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Translucent card over the panel backdrop. It is the one theme that needs
   something behind it to be worth anything, which is why choosing it also
   turns the panel backdrop on -- see the auto mapping in welcome.php.

   No backdrop-filter. What is behind a card is the wash: a few radial
   gradients so soft that blurring them changes nothing you can see. The blur
   still had to be redone for every card on every frame of a scroll, and again
   under any dialog or menu laid over the dashboard -- twenty-odd filtered
   surfaces paying for a difference nobody could point at.

   The tint runs down the card rather than across it, and it is finished by 58%
   -- roughly where the header ends and the first row begins. The card is still
   identifiable by its colour at a glance, from the part of it that carries the
   title and the icon, and the list below reads off plain glass. The first pass
   ran the tint corner to corner and every row sat in it.

   The white inset line along the top edge is what reads as glass rather than
   as a tint: it is the sheet catching light at its own thickness. Kept at .40
   in light and .07 in dark, because on a dark ground the same value reads as a
   seam rather than a highlight. */
[data-widget-theme="glass"] .pg-widget > .card.widget {
  background:
    linear-gradient(180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) var(--pg-fx-top), transparent) 0%,
      transparent 58%),
    rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.58);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) var(--pg-fx-edge), transparent);
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.40),
    0 6px 20px -20px rgba(15, 15, 35, 0.40);
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] [data-widget-theme="glass"] .pg-widget > .card.widget {
  background:
    linear-gradient(180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) var(--pg-fx-top), transparent) 0%,
      transparent 58%),
    rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.07),
    0 8px 24px -22px #000;
}

/* ── Aurora ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Two light sources across the diagonal: the widget's own accent at the top
   left, and a second bloom at the bottom right mixed from it.

   The second hue is derived rather than declared. Twenty-three widgets would
   otherwise need a second colour table that nothing else reads, and every one
   of them would be a chance for a pairing nobody checked. Mixing the accent
   with indigo keeps every pair inside a quarter turn of the wheel, so emerald
   lands on teal, pink lands on violet, and none of it has to be maintained.

   Both stops are the accent mixed INTO the card background, not the accent at
   an alpha over it. Over near black those two are not the same thing: the
   translucent version keeps the hue at full saturation and lays it on the text
   like a filter, while the mixed version is a darker relative of the card --
   the same way the flat theme's #0b1411 is a relative of emerald. That single
   change is most of the difference between the first pass and this one.

   The blooms are also pulled into the corners: 74% by 48%, dying at 58%. At
   the first pass's 88% by 66% they met in the middle of a square card, which
   turned a diagonal of light into a wash over the whole thing. */
[data-widget-theme="aurora"] .pg-widget > .card.widget {
  --pg-accent-shift: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) 55%, #6366f1);
  background:
    radial-gradient(74% 48% at 100% 100%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent-shift) var(--pg-fx-two), var(--bs-card-bg)) 0%,
      transparent 58%),
    radial-gradient(74% 48% at 0% 0%,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) var(--pg-fx-one), var(--bs-card-bg)) 0%,
      transparent 58%),
    var(--bs-card-bg);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent, #64748b) var(--pg-fx-edge), transparent);
}

/* ══ Panel backdrop ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   The panel's content area, not the document body: the wash sits behind the
   widget grid and the icon rail keeps its own colour. Stored in
   dashboard.panel_backdrop, and "auto" is resolved in welcome.php from the card
   theme rather than here, because CSS cannot map one attribute onto another.

   background-attachment: fixed, so the wash reads as light in the room instead
   of as a pattern printed on a page that scrolls past it.

   Every alpha here is under .11 in light and under .17 in dark. These sit
   behind body text and behind the cards' own translucent surfaces; anything
   stronger stops being a backdrop and starts being a competing surface. There
   is no rule for "none" -- absence is the whole behaviour, and it is what the
   flat theme maps to. */
#content[data-panel-backdrop="mesh"],
#content[data-panel-backdrop="dusk"],
#content[data-panel-backdrop="ember"] {
  background-attachment: fixed;
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
}
#content[data-panel-backdrop="mesh"] {
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(58% 42% at 8% 0%,    rgba(124, 58, 237, 0.10), transparent 62%),
    radial-gradient(52% 44% at 94% 34%,  rgba(14, 165, 233, 0.09), transparent 62%),
    radial-gradient(48% 38% at 42% 100%, rgba(236, 72, 153, 0.07), transparent 62%);
}
#content[data-panel-backdrop="dusk"] {
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(62% 46% at 4% 0%,    rgba(79, 70, 229, 0.10), transparent 64%),
    radial-gradient(58% 48% at 100% 78%, rgba(13, 148, 136, 0.08), transparent 64%);
}
#content[data-panel-backdrop="ember"] {
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(60% 44% at 96% 0%, rgba(217, 119, 6, 0.10), transparent 64%),
    radial-gradient(54% 46% at 0% 82%, rgba(225, 29, 72, 0.08), transparent 64%);
}

/* Dark needs more of the same colour, not a different one: the identical alpha
   over near-black reads as nothing at all. */
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] #content[data-panel-backdrop="mesh"] {
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(58% 42% at 8% 0%,    rgba(139, 92, 246, 0.16), transparent 62%),
    radial-gradient(52% 44% at 94% 34%,  rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.13), transparent 62%),
    radial-gradient(48% 38% at 42% 100%, rgba(244, 114, 182, 0.10), transparent 62%);
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] #content[data-panel-backdrop="dusk"] {
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(62% 46% at 4% 0%,    rgba(129, 140, 248, 0.15), transparent 64%),
    radial-gradient(58% 48% at 100% 78%, rgba(45, 212, 191, 0.12), transparent 64%);
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] #content[data-panel-backdrop="ember"] {
  background-image:
    radial-gradient(60% 44% at 96% 0%, rgba(251, 191, 36, 0.14), transparent 64%),
    radial-gradient(54% 46% at 0% 82%, rgba(251, 113, 133, 0.11), transparent 64%);
}

/* ══ Card filters ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   A card body marked data-pg-filterable carries a column of .pg-filter
   buttons and rows tagged data-kind; the page script shows the rows of the
   pressed button's kind (welcome.php). Each button names its kind, says how
   many came in today and, where there is a daily series, draws the week as
   seven bars. Its colour arrives inline as --pg-k (tile) and --pg-k-ink
   (glyph), so one card can carry eight kinds without eight rules here. */
.pg-filter {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  width: 100%;
  min-width: 0;
  padding: 0.35rem 0.45rem;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius-lg);
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  line-height: 1.2;
  text-align: left;
  transition: background-color 0.15s ease;
}
.pg-filter:hover,
.pg-filter:focus-visible {
  background: var(--pg-tint);
}
/* The pressed kind: a tint of its own colour and a short bar on the edge
   facing the list, so it reads as the thing the list is showing. */
.pg-filter[aria-pressed="true"] {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent)) 14%, transparent);
  box-shadow: inset -3px 0 0 var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent));
}
.pg-filter-ico {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 28px;
  height: 28px;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  background: var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent, #64748b));
  color: var(--pg-k-ink, var(--pg-accent-ink, #fff));
  font-size: 14px;
}
.pg-filter-text {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  min-width: 0;
}
.pg-filter-label {
  overflow: hidden;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-filter-sub {
  overflow: hidden;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-filter-count {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  min-width: 1.5rem;
  padding: 0.1rem 0.4rem;
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent)) 18%, transparent);
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  font-weight: 700;
  text-align: center;
}
.pg-filter-count.is-urgent {
  background: var(--pg-danger-bg-subtle);
  color: var(--pg-danger-fg-emphasis);
}
.pg-filter-count.is-clear {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, #10b981 18%, transparent);
}

/* A week of daily counts in seven bars (pg_widget_bars). Plain boxes rather
   than a canvas: a filter column holds up to eight of them and they are
   redrawn with the card. */
.pg-bars {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: flex-end;
  gap: 2px;
  height: 14px;
}
.pg-bars > i {
  width: 3px;
  border-radius: 1px;
  background: var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent));
  opacity: 0.8;
}
.pg-bars > i.is-zero {
  opacity: 0.25;
}

/* The way to the full list of the kind that is showing, under its rows. */
.pg-filter-more {
  display: block;
  margin: 0.25rem 0 0.5rem;
  padding: 0.35rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius-lg);
  color: var(--pg-accent);
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-align: center;
  text-decoration: none;
}
.pg-filter-more:hover,
.pg-filter-more:focus-visible {
  background: var(--pg-tint);
  color: var(--pg-accent);
}
.pg-filter-none .pg-widget-empty {
  padding: 2rem 0.5rem;
}

/* Activity (widget 7): the kinds down the left, the feed on the right. Stacked
   in a narrow card the kinds wrap into a row of chips above the feed, with the
   bars and the second line left out -- a column of eight full-width buttons
   would push the feed below the fold of a phone-width card. */
.pg-act > .pg-filters {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.25rem;
  padding: 0.5rem;
}
.pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter {
  width: auto;
  padding: 0.2rem 0.45rem 0.2rem 0.2rem;
  border: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-rule);
  border-radius: 999px;
}
.pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter[aria-pressed="true"] {
  box-shadow: none;
  border-color: var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent));
}
.pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter-ico {
  width: 20px;
  height: 20px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  font-size: 11px;
}
.pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-bars,
.pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter-sub {
  display: none;
}
@container pgwidget (min-width: 30rem) {
  .pg-split.pg-act > .pg-filters {
    flex: 0 0 36%;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    gap: 2px;
    max-width: 15rem;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.35rem;
  }
  /* 24px tiles and tight padding: eight kinds fit the height of a card
     without the column scrolling. */
  .pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter {
    width: 100%;
    padding: 0.25rem 0.45rem;
    border: 0;
    border-radius: var(--pg-radius-lg);
  }
  .pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter[aria-pressed="true"] {
    box-shadow: inset -3px 0 0 var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent));
  }
  .pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter-ico {
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
    border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
    font-size: 12px;
  }
  .pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-bars {
    display: inline-flex;
  }
  .pg-act > .pg-filters .pg-filter-sub {
    display: block;
  }
}

/* Operations Desk (widget 9): what the shop owes, as three lanes -- to ship,
   to refund, out of stock. The lanes are .pg-filter buttons laid out as
   tiles across the top, each with its count large enough to read from the
   other side of the desk; the pressed lane's list fills the rest of the
   card. In a one-track card the tiles keep their row and drop the second
   line, so the list still starts within the first third of the card. */
.pg-ops {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  min-height: 0;
}
.pg-lanes {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: grid;
  grid-auto-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  grid-auto-flow: column;
  gap: 0.4rem;
  padding: 0.5rem 0.5rem 0.35rem;
}
.pg-lanes .pg-filter {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  grid-template-areas: "ico text count";
  align-items: center;
  column-gap: 0.5rem;
  padding: 0.45rem 0.55rem;
  border: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-rule);
  background: var(--pg-tint);
}
.pg-lanes .pg-filter[aria-pressed="true"] {
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent)) 50%, transparent);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent)) 12%, transparent);
  box-shadow: inset 0 -3px 0 var(--pg-k, var(--pg-accent));
}
.pg-lanes .pg-filter-ico {
  grid-area: ico;
  width: 32px;
  height: 32px;
  font-size: 16px;
}
.pg-lanes .pg-filter-text {
  grid-area: text;
}
.pg-lanes .pg-filter-count {
  grid-area: count;
  min-width: 0;
  padding: 0;
  background: none;
  font-size: 1.5rem;
  line-height: 1;
}
.pg-lanes .pg-filter-count.is-urgent {
  background: none;
  color: var(--pg-danger-fg-emphasis);
}
.pg-lanes .pg-filter-count.is-clear {
  background: none;
  color: var(--pg-success-fg-emphasis);
}
@container pgwidget (max-width: 30rem) {
  .pg-lanes .pg-filter {
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas: "ico count" "text text";
    row-gap: 0.25rem;
    padding: 0.4rem;
  }
  .pg-lanes .pg-filter-ico {
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
    font-size: 12px;
  }
  .pg-lanes .pg-filter-count {
    justify-self: end;
    font-size: 1.15rem;
  }
  .pg-lanes .pg-filter-sub {
    display: none;
  }
}
.pg-ops-body {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 0;
  overflow-x: hidden;
  overflow-y: auto;
}
.pg-ops-meter {
  padding: 0.15rem 1rem 0.1rem;
}

/* Best sellers in the Ecommerce Summary (widget 3): units sold as a quiet
   pill, then what they took. A product without a photo carries its rank on
   the tile instead. */
.pg-ec-qty {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-accent) 16%, transparent);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-size: 0.625rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-rank {
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 700;
}

/* Top pages in Visitor Summaries (widget 5): the list takes the chart's
   place from the strip's last cell, at the same size, so the card does not
   move when it opens. */
.pg-vs-pages {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-height: 4rem;
  margin: 2px -8px 0;
  overflow-x: hidden;
  overflow-y: auto;
}
.pg-vs-pages .pg-list {
  padding-top: 0;
}
.pg-vs-top .pg-vs-cell-label .bi {
  margin-left: 0.15rem;
  opacity: 0.8;
}

/* ══ Dashboard welcome card ════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The greeting and the clock are one card at the top of the dashboard, as wide
   as the widget grid under it and with the same radius and the grid's gap
   below it. They used to be two loose pieces in a row, the brief on the left
   and a large clock pushed to the far right, and the empty middle split the
   row in two.

   Wide, the brief takes the room on the left and the date and time sit on the
   right inside the card, vertically centred and set off by a hairline. Narrow,
   the two stack, brief first, and the time and date share one baseline under
   a hairline across. */
.pg-clockbar {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  margin: 0.25rem 0 2rem;
  padding: 0.85rem 1rem 0.85rem 1.1rem;
}
/* A thin stroke of the clock's gradient down the left edge, inset from the
   corners so it does not have to follow the radius. Positioned rather than
   clipped: overflow on the card would also clip the mark's ring. */
.pg-clockbar::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  top: 0.75rem;
  bottom: 0.75rem;
  width: 3px;
  border-radius: 3px;
  background: var(--clock-gradient);
  pointer-events: none;
}
/* The glass card theme lays the widgets over the panel backdrop; the welcome
   card follows them so it does not read as the one opaque sheet on the page. */
[data-widget-theme="glass"] .pg-clockbar {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.58);
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] [data-widget-theme="glass"] .pg-clockbar {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
}
.pg-clockbar > .pg-clockbar-now {
  padding-top: 0.75rem;
  border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-border-color) 70%, transparent);
}
@media (min-width: 768px) {
  .pg-clockbar {
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1rem 1.5rem 1rem 1.25rem;
  }
  .pg-clockbar > .pg-brief {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
  }
  /* Parent-qualified: the base rules for these two come further down. */
  .pg-clockbar > .pg-clockbar-now {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    align-self: stretch;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: flex-end;
    max-width: 40%;
    padding: 0 0 0 1.5rem;
    border-top: 0;
    border-left: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-border-color) 70%, transparent);
    text-align: right;
  }
  .pg-clockbar .pg-clockbar-when {
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: 0.15rem;
  }
}
/* The briefing.
   A mark and a short paragraph, laid out the way a remark is laid out rather
   than the way a stat line is: the chip reads as whoever is speaking, and the
   greeting and the line after it run on as one piece of prose instead of being
   separated by a bullet. Weight does the separating -- the greeting is the
   darker half -- which costs no punctuation and no second row.

   The sentence is capped at 62ch. Prose set across two thousand pixels is not
   read, it is scanned, and the whole point of writing this as a sentence is
   that it gets read.

   The card around it is the welcome card (.pg-clockbar); the brief itself
   has no surface of its own. */
.pg-brief {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 0.6rem;
  min-width: 0;
}
/* Grows, so "Say something else" sits at the card's reading end rather than
   wherever the sentence happens to stop. */
.pg-brief-body {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.4rem;
  min-width: 0;
}

/* "Say something else": a quiet round button at the end of the card, the
   same size as the mark so the two ends of the card balance. It turns while
   the new line is on its way. */
.pg-brief-again {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 1.75rem;
  height: 1.75rem;
  margin: 0 -0.35rem 0 0.1rem;
  padding: 0;
  border: 0;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--pg-fg-3);
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  line-height: 1;
  transition: color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
}
.pg-brief-again:hover,
.pg-brief-again:focus-visible {
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  background: var(--pg-bg-2);
}
.pg-brief-again .bi {
  display: inline-block;
}
.pg-brief-again .bi::before {
  vertical-align: 0;
}
.pg-brief-again.is-busy .bi {
  animation: pg-brief-spin 0.8s linear infinite;
}
@keyframes pg-brief-spin {
  to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}

/* The speaker. It carries the hour's own glyph -- sunrise, sun, dusk, moon --
   so the mark is telling you something too, and takes the clock's gradient so
   the two halves of this bar are visibly one object. */
.pg-brief-mark {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 1.75rem;
  height: 1.75rem;
  margin-top: 0;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--clock-gradient);
  box-shadow: 0 1px 6px var(--clock-glow);
  color: #fff;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  line-height: 1;
}
.pg-brief-mark .bi::before {
  vertical-align: 0;
  line-height: 1;
}

.pg-brief-text {
  max-width: 62ch;
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  line-height: 1.5;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
/* The half with a name in it. */
.pg-brief-greet {
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-brief-line {
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}

/* The way to whatever the sentence is about.
   Inside the sentence, on the words themselves -- "the 2 empty products" -- and
   not on a chip parked at the end. A chip is a second thing to read that says
   the same as the phrase before it, and it makes you look away from the words
   to find the target. Here the thing named and the thing clicked are one.

   Underlined rather than coloured: the line around it is already secondary ink
   and a third colour in one sentence is a decoration, not a signal. */
.pg-brief-link {
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-weight: 600;
  text-decoration: underline;
  text-decoration-color: var(--pg-border-color);
  text-underline-offset: 0.15em;
}
.pg-brief-link:hover,
.pg-brief-link:focus-visible {
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  text-decoration-color: currentColor;
}
/* It arrives rather than being there, which is most of what makes it read as
   something said. A transform and an opacity, so nothing reflows while it
   plays -- and nothing at all for anyone who has asked for less movement. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .pg-brief-text {
    animation: pg-brief-in 0.45s ease-out both;
  }
  /* The strip follows the sentence rather than arriving with it, so the eye
     lands on the words first. */
  .pg-brief-ws {
    animation: pg-brief-in 0.45s ease-out 0.18s both;
  }
  /* One soft ring from the mark as the line arrives, once, and then still. */
  .pg-brief-mark {
    animation: pg-brief-ring 0.9s ease-out 1;
  }
  /* A new line asked for with the button arrives the same way. The second
     set of keyframes is what restarts it: an element keeps a running
     animation for as long as its name stays the same. */
  .pg-brief.is-new .pg-brief-text {
    animation: pg-brief-again 0.45s ease-out both;
  }
  .pg-brief.is-new .pg-brief-mark {
    animation: pg-brief-ring-again 0.9s ease-out 1;
  }
}
@keyframes pg-brief-in {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(2px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}
@keyframes pg-brief-ring {
  from { box-shadow: 0 1px 6px var(--clock-glow), 0 0 0 0 var(--clock-glow); }
  to   { box-shadow: 0 1px 6px var(--clock-glow), 0 0 0 0.55rem transparent; }
}
@keyframes pg-brief-again {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(2px); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: none; }
}
@keyframes pg-brief-ring-again {
  from { box-shadow: 0 1px 6px var(--clock-glow), 0 0 0 0 var(--clock-glow); }
  to   { box-shadow: 0 1px 6px var(--clock-glow), 0 0 0 0.55rem transparent; }
}

/* The workspace strip: what was handed over, what is under way, what is late.
   Small pills under the sentence, the same few facts in the same place on
   every load -- the glance, where the sentence is the read. */
.pg-brief-ws {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.35rem 0.5rem;
}
.pg-brief-ws-item {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.35rem;
  max-width: 100%;
  padding: 0.15rem 0.55rem 0.15rem 0.45rem;
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-border-color) 60%, transparent);
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-bg-2) 80%, transparent);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
  text-decoration: none;
  --pg-lift-also: border-color 0.15s ease;
}
.pg-brief-ws-item:hover,
.pg-brief-ws-item:focus-visible {
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 45%, transparent);
}
/* Something new: a small dot in the clock's colours ahead of the icon. */
.pg-brief-ws-item.is-new::before {
  content: "";
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 6px;
  height: 6px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--clock-gradient);
}
.pg-brief-ws-item.is-late .bi,
.pg-brief-ws-item.is-late .pg-brief-ws-label {
  color: var(--pg-danger-fg-emphasis);
}
/* A timer that is running: a green dot that breathes, so the one item that
   goes on counting by itself looks alive. */
.pg-brief-ws-item.is-live::before {
  content: "";
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 6px;
  height: 6px;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: var(--pg-success);
}
.pg-brief-ws-item.is-live .bi {
  color: var(--pg-success);
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .pg-brief-ws-item.is-live::before {
    animation: pg-live 1.6s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
}
@keyframes pg-live {
  0%, 100% { opacity: 1; }
  50%      { opacity: 0.3; }
}
.pg-brief-ws-label {
  font-weight: 600;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-brief-ws-text {
  min-width: 0;
  max-width: 22ch;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-brief-ws-bar {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 3.5rem;
  height: 3px;
  border-radius: 3px;
  background: var(--pg-border-color);
  overflow: hidden;
}
.pg-brief-ws-bar > span {
  display: block;
  height: 100%;
  background: var(--clock-gradient);
}
.pg-brief-ws-pct {
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  font-weight: 600;
}
@media (max-width: 575.98px) {
  .pg-brief-ws-text {
    max-width: 14ch;
  }
}
.pg-clockbar-now {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 0.35rem;
  min-width: 0;
}
.pg-clockbar-when {
  display: flex;
  align-items: baseline;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0 0.7rem;
}
.pg-clockbar-time {
  font-size: 1.75rem;
  line-height: 1.05;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: -0.01em;
  /* The digits are replaced every minute by the clock poll; proportional
     figures would shift the date beside them whenever a 1 swaps for a 0. */
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  color: var(--clock-fallback-color);
}
/* Progressive enhancement: gradient text where clipping is supported. */
@supports ((-webkit-background-clip: text) or (background-clip: text)) {
  .pg-clockbar-time {
    background: var(--clock-gradient);
    color: transparent;
    -webkit-background-clip: text; /* Chrome/Safari/Edge */
    background-clip: text;          /* Standard (Firefox & modern browsers) */
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    /* drop-shadow, not text-shadow: the fill is transparent, so a text shadow
       would sit behind the glyphs and read through them as a smudge instead of
       haloing them. A filter blooms what was actually painted. */
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 var(--clock-bloom) var(--clock-glow));
  }
}
.pg-clockbar-date {
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--pg-fg-3);
}

/* The next meeting or visit, under the clock: when, what, and how long until
   it starts. Every event left today is in the markup and only the first is
   shown; the page script removes one when it ends. */
.pg-clockbar-next {
  display: flex;
  max-width: 100%;
}
.pg-next + .pg-next {
  display: none;
}
.pg-next {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.4rem;
  max-width: 100%;
  padding: 0.15rem 0.6rem 0.15rem 0.5rem;
  border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-border-color) 60%, transparent);
  border-radius: 999px;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-bg-body) 72%, transparent);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
  text-decoration: none;
  --pg-lift-also: border-color 0.15s ease;
}
html[data-bs-theme="dark"] .pg-next {
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-bg-body) 55%, transparent);
}
.pg-next:hover,
.pg-next:focus-visible {
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  border-color: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 45%, transparent);
}
.pg-next .bi {
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-next-time {
  font-weight: 600;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-next-title {
  min-width: 0;
  max-width: 22ch;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-next-until {
  font-weight: 600;
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  white-space: nowrap;
  color: var(--clock-fallback-color);
}
.pg-next-until:empty {
  display: none;
}
/* Under way: the countdown becomes "now" with the timer's breathing dot. */
.pg-next.is-now .pg-next-until {
  color: var(--pg-success);
}
.pg-next.is-now .pg-next-until::before {
  content: "";
  display: inline-block;
  width: 6px;
  height: 6px;
  margin-right: 0.3rem;
  border-radius: 50%;
  background: currentColor;
  vertical-align: 0.1em;
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  .pg-next.is-now .pg-next-until::before {
    animation: pg-live 1.6s ease-in-out infinite;
  }
}
@media (max-width: 575.98px) {
  .pg-clockbar-time {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
  }
}

/* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Navbar brand: the mascot's window and the Pinegrap wordmark
   ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Layout-shift notes — every rule here earns its place:
   - The lockup is inline-flex with line-height:1 so nothing sits on a text
     baseline and pushes the 50px navbar row around.
   - The mascot (a muted loop over its still poster) and the inline wordmark
     carry explicit sizes from one variable, so the box is known before they
     paint (no CLS).
   - The wordmark's view box keeps room above the letters as large as the
     descenders below, so centring it centres the letters on the mascot.
   - Below md the header shows the mascot alone: a narrow place gets the
     square, a wide one the whole lockup.
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
.pinegrap-logo-lockup {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  vertical-align: middle;
  line-height: 1;
}

/* Only knob worth touching: --pg-logo-h, the mascot's side (32px in the
   header); the wordmark follows. A wrapper sets it for a larger lockup
   (installer, theme designer, loading curtain). */
.pinegrap-logo {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: calc(var(--pg-logo-h, 32px) * 0.22);
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  height: var(--pg-logo-h, 32px);
  line-height: 0;
}

/* A square video (the loop, its poster): the window's corners are rounded
   here, at the mascot drawing's 116/512. */
.pinegrap-logo-mark {
  display: block;
  flex: none;
  width: var(--pg-logo-h, 32px);
  height: var(--pg-logo-h, 32px);
  border-radius: 22.6%;
  object-fit: cover;
  background: #0E1440;
}

.pinegrap-logo-word {
  display: block;
  flex: none;
}

/* viewBox 958 x 375: the letters' height is 0.62 of the mascot's side. */
.pinegrap-logo-word > svg {
  display: block;
  height: calc(var(--pg-logo-h, 32px) * 1.345);
  width: calc(var(--pg-logo-h, 32px) * 3.436);
}

/* Below md the lockup no longer fits beside the menu button and the right
   hand cluster: the mascot stays as the square logo, the wordmark and the
   page heading give way, so the controls keep the row. From md up nothing
   changes. */
@media (max-width: 767.98px) {
  #header .pinegrap-logo-word,
  #header .pg-navhead {
    display: none;
  }

  #header .pg-brand-item > a {
    padding-left: 4px;
    padding-right: 4px;
  }
}

/* The quick note beside the bell takes another 44px, which on a phone
   pushed the logo off the bar (#11187). Below sm it gives way: the notes are
   one tap away under My Notes in the menu. */
@media (max-width: 575.98px) {
  #header .pg-quick-note-nav {
    display: none;
  }
}

/* The row of controls needs 348px; the mascot adds 40. Below 390px it gives
   way too rather than push the site link off the bar. */
@media (max-width: 389.98px) {
  #header .pg-brand-item {
    display: none;
  }
}

/* ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   WCAG 2.1 AA baseline
   ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */

/* Skip-to-content link: visually hidden until focused. */
.pg-skip-link {
  position: fixed;
  top: -100px;
  left: 8px;
  z-index: 2000;
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  background: var(--pg-bg-body);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  border: 2px solid var(--pg-primary-ink);
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  text-decoration: none;
  font-weight: 600;
  transition: top 0.15s ease-in-out;
}
.pg-skip-link:focus,
.pg-skip-link:focus-visible {
  top: 8px;
  outline: var(--pg-focus-ring-width) solid var(--pg-primary-ink);
  outline-offset: var(--pg-focus-ring-offset);
}

/* Ensure a visible focus ring on every interactive element.
   Bootstrap covers most cases; this hardens custom widgets and any
   element where focus-visible was suppressed by inline styles. The ring is
   Bootstrap 6's shape (one outline, the token width, the token offset out)
   in the primary ink, which keeps 3:1 against the page where Bootstrap's
   pale ring colour does not. Its outer edge sits 4px out, the room the
   scrolling strips leave for it. */
a:focus-visible,
button:focus-visible,
input:focus-visible,
select:focus-visible,
textarea:focus-visible,
[tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"]):focus-visible,
.btn:focus-visible,
.nav-link:focus-visible,
.menu-item:focus-visible,
.breadcrumb-item a:focus-visible,
.form-adorn:has(> .form-ghost:focus-visible),
.chip-input.pg-chips:has(> .form-ghost:focus-visible) {
  outline: var(--pg-focus-ring-width) solid var(--pg-primary-ink);
  outline-offset: var(--pg-focus-ring-offset);
}
/* A form-adorn field wears the ring on its wrapper, the label drawn as the
   input; the borderless input inside it shows none of its own. */
.form-adorn > .form-ghost:focus-visible {
  outline: 0;
  box-shadow: none;
}

/* Some legacy admin links carry inline `outline:0` style; force visible focus. */
a.no-popover:focus-visible,
button.no-popover:focus-visible {
  outline: var(--pg-focus-ring-width) solid var(--pg-primary-ink) !important;
  outline-offset: var(--pg-focus-ring-offset);
}


/* ── Admin screen toolbar ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The house pattern for a management screen: everything the screen can do
   sits in ONE bar under the page header, and the content below it stays
   plain. First built inside view_folders.php (the file manager) and lifted
   here so every screen can wear it instead of re-inventing a bar of its own -
   which is how settings.php ended up with six different button recipes.

   Markup:

     <div class="pg-toolbar">
       <div class="dropdown">…primary: btn btn-sm btn-primary rounded-pill px-3…</div>
       <button class="btn btn-sm btn-text theme-secondary">…secondary tools…</button>
       <div class="pg-toolbar-grow">…breadcrumb / title / location…</div>
       <div class="input-group input-group-sm rounded-pill pg-toolbar-search">…</div>
       <div class="btn-group btn-group-sm">…view switches: btn-text theme-secondary + .active…</div>
       <div class="dropdown ms-auto">…overflow: btn-text theme-secondary with bi-three-dots-vertical…</div>
     </div>

   Rules that go with it: one recipe per intent, more than four buttons go
   in a menu, icons always inside <i class="bi …">. */

.pg-toolbar,
#button_bar {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .35rem;
  padding: .5rem .75rem;
  border-bottom: 1px solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  min-width: 0;
  max-width: 100%;
}

/* What the page bar takes from the toolbar and what it leaves.
   The explorer's toolbar is the top edge of a panel that runs to the window, so
   the side padding and the rule under it are edges of that panel. The page bar
   has no panel: it sits in the page gutter under the heading, where the padding
   would start the first action three quarters of a rem right of the cards below
   it, and the rule would just be a line drawn across the page.

   It sits here rather than with the rest of #button_bar because it is a
   correction to the block above and has to come after it -- the two selectors
   weigh the same, so the later one wins. */
#button_bar {
  padding-left: 0;
  padding-right: 0;
  border-bottom: 0;
  margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
}

/* Menus inside the bar anchor to their own button, not to the page, so a
   dropdown near the right edge does not push the layout sideways. */
.pg-toolbar .pg-menu-wrap,
#button_bar .pg-menu-wrap { position: relative; }
.pg-toolbar .menu,
#button_bar .menu {
  position: absolute;
  top: 100%;
  left: 0;
  margin-top: .25rem;
  z-index: 1075;

  /* A long menu is not allowed to run off the bottom of the window, and it
     cannot be trusted to stay inside a panel that clips its overflow either -
     the file manager's app shell does exactly that. It scrolls instead. */
  max-height: calc(100vh - 10rem);
  overflow-y: auto;

  /* The wrapper is only as wide as its icon button, and an absolute box
     shrinks to its containing block, so without a width the menu falls back
     to its 10rem minimum and long items scroll sideways inside it. It takes
     its longest item's width instead, up to the window; past that the items
     wrap. */
  width: max-content;
  max-width: min(24rem, calc(100vw - 2rem));
  overflow-x: hidden;
}
.pg-toolbar .menu .menu-item,
#button_bar .menu .menu-item { white-space: normal; }
.pg-toolbar .menu.pg-menu-end,
#button_bar .menu.pg-menu-end { left: auto; right: 0; }

/* The one element that takes the slack - a breadcrumb, a screen title, a
   filter summary. Everything else keeps its natural width. */
.pg-toolbar-grow { flex: 1 1 220px; min-width: 160px; overflow: hidden; }

.pg-toolbar-search { width: 200px; }
/* Backup Manager: the name field and its button share the box. */
.pg-toolbar-search.pg-backup-create { width: 320px; }

/* A filter form (pg_filter_select(), Browse Items' folder and access pickers)
   is a flex item of the bar, and a flex item's automatic minimum is its
   content: a select is as wide as its longest option, so one long folder name
   or report rule pushed the page sideways on a phone. Capped here, the select
   shrinks to the bar and cuts its text instead. */
.pg-toolbar-filter,
.pg-toolbar-filter > .input-group,
.pg-toolbar-filter select {
  min-width: 0;
  max-width: 100%;
}
.pg-toolbar-filter select { text-overflow: ellipsis; }

/* Ghost button: a toolbar full of outlined buttons reads as a row of boxes,
   so secondary tools carry no border until they are hovered. Bootstrap 6's
   text button in the secondary theme is that object; its tokens are set to
   the panel's ink and surfaces, so it follows the light and dark themes:
   - colours: the panel's secondary ink and hover surface, not the theme's;
   - focus: the panel-wide focus ring above still wins over the theme's;
   - disabled: left undefined, so the label keeps the parent's ink and the
     border falls back to it.

   The house rule for a button with no variant on it (the long :not() chain
   further up) has to exclude this one by name. That selector carries a class
   for every :not() in it, so it outweighs these two classes many times over,
   and this block would never take effect otherwise. */
.btn-text.theme-secondary {
  --bs-btn-color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  --bs-btn-hover-color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  --bs-btn-hover-bg: var(--pg-bg-2);
  --bs-btn-active-color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  --bs-btn-active-bg: var(--pg-bg-2);
  --bs-btn-border-color: transparent;
  --bs-btn-hover-border-color: transparent;
  --bs-btn-active-border-color: transparent;
  --bs-btn-disabled-color: initial;
  --bs-btn-disabled-border-color: initial;
}

/* A view switch that is currently on. Not :active - that is the pressed
   state and lasts a moment; this one has to hold. */
.btn-text.theme-secondary.active {
  background: var(--pg-bg-2);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
}

/* Narrow screens: the two elements that actually need width - the location
   and the search box - each get a line of their own. Without this the location
   gets about two hundred pixels to say where you are, which is the one thing
   it cannot do in two hundred pixels.

   They take that line where they stand in the markup, not at the bottom of the
   bar: moving them with `order` would draw them below controls that Tab and a
   screen reader still reach after them, so focus would jump down and climb
   back up. A grow left empty is only a spacer pushing the rest to the right;
   on a full line it would split the bar in two, so it is hidden here. */
@media (max-width: 767.98px) {

  .pg-toolbar,
  #button_bar { row-gap: .45rem; }

  .pg-toolbar-grow,
  .pg-toolbar-search {
    flex: 1 1 100%;
    width: auto !important;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .pg-toolbar-grow:empty { display: none; }
}

/* All Orders (view_orders.php). The bar: a rule before the period navigator;
   under 1280 px the rule becomes a line break, so the navigator starts the
   bar's second row with the controls after it, in markup order. A sparkline
   at the right of its figure: Chart.js draws with maintainAspectRatio off, so
   the box sets the size. */
.pg-orders-bar-rule {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  align-self: center;
  width: 0;
  height: 20px;
  margin: 0 4px;
  border-left: 1px solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
}
.pg-orders-period {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 4px;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
}
.pg-orders-period > .text-nowrap {
  margin: 0 4px;
  font-size: var(--pg-text-2);
  font-weight: var(--pg-weight-strong);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
}
@media (max-width: 1279.98px) {
  .pg-orders-bar-rule {
    flex-basis: 100%;
    height: 0;
    margin: 0;
    border: 0;
  }
}
.pg-orders-spark { position: relative; flex: 1 1 0; min-width: 48px; max-width: 96px; height: 28px; }
/* Sort, the selects and the filters button wrap as one group pushed to the
   right (no grow slot: its minimum width would wrap the group a row lower). */
.pg-orders-bar-end {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .35rem;
  margin-left: auto;
  min-width: 0;
  max-width: 100%;
}
/* The status select keeps the bar one row at 1440 px: at most 200 px, its
   text cut with an ellipsis (.pg-toolbar-filter select); the open list shows
   the whole text. The utility w-auto sets the width, so the cap is a
   max-width. */
.pg-orders-bar-end #status { max-width: 200px; }
/* On a phone Sort, the status select, the type select and the filters button
   share one row, the status select taking the free width; Sort stays first
   in its markup place, so Tab follows the rows. */
@media (max-width: 575.98px) {
  .pg-orders-bar-end {
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
    width: 100%;
  }
  .pg-orders-bar-end > .pg-toolbar-filter:has(#status) {
    flex: 1 1 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .pg-orders-bar-end #status {
    min-width: 100%;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
}
/* The quick filters stand under the bar, above the figure strip. */
.pg-orders-quick { margin: 0 0 12px; }
/* A change note stands between a figure and its sparkline, in the figure
   grid's change inks. */
.pg-figure-strip-line > .pg-figure-note {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  margin: 2px 0 0;
}
.pg-figure-strip-line > .pg-figure-note.is-good {
  color: var(--pg-success-fg-emphasis);
  font-weight: var(--pg-weight-medium);
}
.pg-figure-strip-line > .pg-figure-note.is-bad {
  font-weight: var(--pg-weight-medium);
}
/* Customer and Products give way so the default columns fit beside the open
   Filters panel: one line, cut with an ellipsis, the whole text as the
   title (pg_order_list_clip_html()). */
.pg-orders-clip {
  display: block;
  max-width: 180px;
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-orders-summary-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
}
.pg-orders-summary-part {
  min-width: 0;
  padding: 14px 18px 16px;
}
.pg-orders-summary-part + .pg-orders-summary-part {
  border-left: 1px solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
}
.pg-orders-summary-part > .pg-group-label {
  margin: 0 0 6px;
}
.pg-orders-summary-bars {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}
.pg-orders-summary-bars > li {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 170px) minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  gap: 10px;
  align-items: center;
  margin-top: 7px;
  font-size: var(--pg-text-2);
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-orders-summary-name {
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-orders-summary-track > i {
  display: block;
  height: 10px;
  border-radius: 5px;
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-primary) 70%, transparent);
}
.pg-orders-summary-value {
  min-width: 28px;
  text-align: right;
  font-weight: var(--pg-weight-strong);
  font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
}
@media (max-width: 767.98px) {
  .pg-orders-summary-grid {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
  }
  .pg-orders-summary-part + .pg-orders-summary-part {
    border-left: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  }
  .pg-orders-summary-bars > li {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 40%) minmax(0, 1fr) auto;
  }
}

/* The design list's shared components panel carries a six column table;
   Bootstrap's 400px offcanvas would wrap every cell. */
#sd-comp-offcanvas { --pg-drawer-width: min(100vw, 860px); }

/* A pick wave's pick list (pick_wave.php): each row is the tick's label, the
   shelf address at its left in the figure type, the units at its right. A
   ticked row is dimmed (.is-read) and moved to the end by pick_waves.js.
   On a phone the whole row stays a large tick target. */
.pg-pick-card { padding: 14px 16px; }
.pg-pick-bar { margin: 0 0 2px; font-weight: var(--pg-weight-strong); }
.pg-pick-rows { margin: 10px -12px 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; }
.pg-pick-row { align-items: center; }
.pg-pick-row > .pg-checkbox { flex: none; }
.pg-pick-label { display: flex; flex: 1; align-items: center; gap: 14px; min-width: 0; min-height: 48px; margin: 0; cursor: pointer; }
.pg-pick-bin { flex: none; min-width: 6.5rem; margin: 0; }
.pg-pick-image { flex: none; width: 48px; height: 48px; border-radius: 8px; object-fit: cover; }
.pg-pick-units { display: flex; flex: none; flex-direction: column; align-items: flex-end; text-align: right; }
.pg-pick-units > .pg-figure-value { margin: 0; }
.pg-pick-row.is-read .pg-pick-bin,
.pg-pick-row.is-read .pg-pick-image,
.pg-pick-row.is-read .pg-pick-units { opacity: .45; }
@media (max-width: 575.98px) {
  .pg-pick-label { flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 6px 12px; }
  .pg-pick-bin { min-width: 0; }
  .pg-pick-row .pg-list-row-main { flex-basis: 100%; order: 3; }
}

/* The Pack screen (pack.php): the scan field's row turns red for a refused
   scan (.is-refused, two seconds, pack.js); each line is a card, a full one
   turns green (.is-full) and sinks to the end. */
.pg-pack { max-width: 44rem; margin: 0 auto; }
.pg-pack-scan-row { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: center; }
.pg-pack-code { flex: 1; min-width: 0; min-height: 48px; }
.pg-pack-scan.is-refused .pg-pack-code { border-color: var(--pg-tone-rose); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-tone-rose) 12%, transparent); }
.pg-pack-scan.is-refused .pg-field-help { color: var(--pg-tone-rose); }
.pg-pack-video { width: 100%; margin-top: 8px; aspect-ratio: 4 / 3; object-fit: cover; border-radius: 12px; }
.pg-pack-choices { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; }
.pg-pack-lines { margin: 0; padding: 0; list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
.pg-pack-line { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 14px; padding: 12px 16px; }
.pg-pack-line .pg-list-row-main { display: flex; flex-direction: column; flex: 1; min-width: 0; }
.pg-pack-units { flex: none; margin: 0; }
.pg-pack-line.is-full { border-color: var(--pg-tone-teal); background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-tone-teal) 10%, transparent); }
.pg-pack-foot { display: flex; gap: 8px; justify-content: flex-end; margin-top: 16px; }
.pg-pack-done { margin: 12px 0 16px; padding: 0; list-style: none; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; }
.pg-pack-done > li { display: flex; gap: 8px; align-items: baseline; flex-wrap: wrap; }
.pg-pack-ok { color: var(--pg-tone-teal); }
.pg-pack-warn { color: var(--pg-tone-amber); }
.pg-pack-short { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; align-items: center; }
.pg-pack-found { flex: none; width: 6rem; }

/* ── Settings screen section anchors ──────────────────────────────────────
   The settings cards carry an id (#pgset-security, #pgset-waf, …) so the
   global search can link straight to a section of that very long screen.
   The screen has a sticky header, so without this the browser parks the
   card title underneath it and the operator lands on a heading they cannot
   read. The value is the header height plus a little air.

   The same applies to #pgsub- ids, the jump points inside the longer sections,
   and to the product screen's #pg_pb_sec_ ids -- all three are reached from a
   rail the same way and land under the header for the same reason.

   The product screen's #pg_pb_sec_ ids scroll the window, where the html
   scroll padding below already clears the top bar; their margin only adds the
   air, or the two would stack. */
[id^="pgset-"],
[id^="pgsub-"] { scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
[id^="pg_pb_sec_"] { scroll-margin-top: 1.5rem; }

/* ── Window scroll padding ────────────────────────────────────────────────
   The panel scrolls the window under the fixed, opaque top bar, so whatever
   the browser scrolls into view - the field Shift+Tab reaches, the target of
   a #fragment, the skip link's #content - was parked at y=0 behind the bar.
   Scroll padding on the root scroller moves that line below the bar.

   At the bottom, the sticky save bar and the marketplace
   bulk bar cover the control Tab brings in, so the bottom line clears them;
   on a phone the save bar wraps two actions per row and, beside the chat
   bubble, sits --pg-chat-clearance higher (that token lives on the bar, so
   the root reads its fallback). Each predicate is its own :has(): a :has()
   inside a :has() is invalid and drops the whole rule. */
html {
  scroll-padding-top: calc(var(--pg-navbar-h) + 0.5rem);
}
html:has(nav.buttons.navigation.position-sticky),
html:has(.position-sticky > nav.buttons.navigation) {
  scroll-padding-bottom: 4rem;
}
html:has(.mpp-bulk:not(.d-none)) {
  scroll-padding-bottom: 5rem;
}
@media (max-width: 575.98px) {
  html:has(nav.buttons.navigation.position-sticky),
  html:has(.position-sticky > nav.buttons.navigation) {
    scroll-padding-bottom: 7rem;
  }
  html:has(#pg-chat-root):has(nav.buttons.navigation.position-sticky),
  html:has(#pg-chat-root):has(.position-sticky > nav.buttons.navigation) {
    scroll-padding-bottom: calc(7rem + var(--pg-chat-clearance, 5.125rem));
  }
  html:has(.mpp-bulk:not(.d-none)) {
    scroll-padding-bottom: 7.5rem;
  }
}


/* ── Admin screen stepper with cards ──────────────────────────────────────
   Bootstrap's .stepper down the left edge of a screen whose sections are read
   in order, top to bottom (edit_offer.php), each step a card. The stepper
   sizes its rows evenly and its text column to the content, so a tall card
   would stretch every row and a wide one overflow; here rows take their own
   height, the card column the rest of the width, and the number sits level
   with the card header. Every item is .active (only those are coloured);
   its theme class carries the state, and .theme-reset on the card keeps that
   theme out of the card's own buttons and switches. */

.pg-stepper-cards {
  --bs-stepper-size: 1.75rem;
  --bs-stepper-gap: .75rem;
  --bs-stepper-text-gap: 1rem;
  --bs-stepper-align-items: start;
  grid-auto-rows: auto;
}
.pg-stepper-cards > .stepper-item { grid-template-columns: var(--bs-stepper-size) minmax(0, 1fr); }
/* A step that holds more than one child (a title, its text, a picture, a
   button) keeps them all in the content column; the grid would otherwise
   fill the number column with every second child. */
.pg-stepper-cards > .stepper-item > * { grid-column: 2; }
.pg-stepper-cards > .stepper-item::before { margin-top: .6rem; }
.pg-stepper-cards > .stepper-item::after {
  inset-block-start: calc(.6rem + var(--bs-stepper-size) * .5);
  height: calc(100% + var(--bs-stepper-gap));
}

/* ══ Settings screen ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   The layout is Bootstrap's: a row, a column for the rail, a column for the
   sections. What is here is the rail's own appearance, the order the sections
   are read in, and a type scale -- nothing that the grid already does. */
.pg-settings-nav {
  position: sticky;
  /* Against the navbar's height token rather than a number copied from it, so
     the rail keeps its distance if the header ever changes. */
  top: calc(var(--pg-navbar-h) + 0.75rem);
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 1px;
  max-height: calc(100vh - var(--pg-navbar-h) - 2rem);
  overflow-y: auto;
  /* The rail scrolls, so it clips: inner room for the full-width links'
     focus ring, given back by the margin so the rail does not move. */
  padding: 4px;
  margin: -4px;
}
.pg-settings-group {
  padding: 0.75rem 0.5rem 0.25rem;
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-settings-group:first-child {
  padding-top: 0;
}
.pg-settings-link {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  padding: 0.375rem 0.5rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  line-height: 1.3;
  text-decoration: none;
}
.pg-settings-link i {
  flex: 0 0 1rem;
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  text-align: center;
  opacity: 0.7;
}
.pg-settings-link span {
  overflow: hidden;
  white-space: nowrap;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
.pg-settings-link:hover,
.pg-settings-link:focus-visible {
  background: var(--pg-tint);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
}
/* The section under the reading line. Marked by weight, a tint and an edge
   rather than by a fill: the rail sits beside twelve cards that all carry
   tints of their own, and a filled row would read as a thirteenth. */
.pg-settings-link.is-active {
  background: var(--pg-tint-strong);
  box-shadow: inset 2px 0 0 var(--pg-primary);
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-settings-link.is-active i {
  opacity: 1;
  color: var(--pg-primary);
}

/* Jump points inside the commerce section. Indented to the icon column of the
   rows above them, so the indent alone says these belong to the section they
   sit under rather than standing beside it. */
.pg-settings-subs {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 1px;
}
.pg-settings-sublink {
  padding-left: 2rem;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-settings-sublink:hover,
.pg-settings-sublink:focus-visible {
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
}

/* A notch smaller through the whole screen: twelve sections of settings is
   a dense page whatever the layout. The card titles and the descriptions
   are look A's ("Look A: settings"). */
.pg-settings-body .form-label {
  margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
}
.pg-settings-body :is(.form-field > label),
.pg-settings-body :is(select.form-control),
.pg-settings-body .form-control {
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
}
.pg-settings-body h6 {
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
}
/* A card's introduction paragraph reads at the size of a description, and a
   legend used as a field name at the size of a name: at body size (16px) the
   accounting cards that carry them looked larger than every other card.
   Outside the settings the legend keeps the 1rem it had from fs-md. */
.pg-settings-body .pg-set-card p.fg-secondary {
  font-size: 0.75rem;
}
legend.form-label {
  font-size: 1rem;
}
/* A setting's name reads the same wherever its control sits: a name above a
   picture field, a text area, a tag field or a group of choices is the name
   of a setting too, and at 13px regular it read as a caption beside the
   14px bold names of the rows around it. The choices inside a group keep
   their own smaller labels; a drawer's form keeps its own. */
:is(.pg-settings-body, #pg_settings_sections) .pg-set-card .form-label:not(.pg-set-drawer *, .drawer *) {
  font-size: var(--pg-text-base);
  font-weight: var(--pg-weight-strong);
}

/* Section rail placement, shared by every screen that has one.
   <main> becomes two columns from xl up: the rail, then everything the page
   drew. Below xl it is an ordinary block again and the page is untouched --
   which is why the wrapper exists at all, so there is one thing to switch on
   rather than a layout per screen. */
@media (min-width: 1280px) {
  main.pg-has-rail {
    display: flex;
    /* No align-items here on purpose. The rail has to be as tall as the page
       beside it, because the nav inside it is sticky and a sticky box inside a
       box its own size has nowhere to travel -- it scrolls away with the form.
       flex-start sizes the item to its contents and is exactly what breaks it;
       stretch is the default and is the one that is wanted. */
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  main.pg-has-rail > .pg-settings-menu {
    flex: 0 0 13rem;
    width: 13rem;
  }
  main.pg-has-rail > .pg-page-body {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-width: 0;
  }
}
/* Below the split the rail is a panel under the header, the same one the
   settings screen has always used. */
@media (max-width: 1279.98px) {
  main.pg-has-rail > .pg-settings-menu {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }
}

/* The rail arrives after the first paint: the shell renders the slot hidden and
   the script lifts it out beside the page once the DOM is there. Without this
   the screen drew full width and then lost 232px of it in one frame, and every
   line of the form slid sideways under the eye after it was already readable.

   So the space is booked here from markup the server already knows --
   .pg-awaiting-rail goes on .software-content for any screen that asked for a
   rail -- and initSectionRail drops the class in the same breath as it does the
   move. The content box lands on the same x either way, so there is nothing to
   see. Measured: drift is 0px both axes with this, 232px and 16px without.

   Written through #content rather than a class pair because #content carries a
   padding of its own from an id selector, and a class pair loses to it -- the
   reservation was silently doing nothing until this was keyed the same way. */
@media (min-width: 1280px) {
  .software-content.pg-awaiting-rail > #content {
    padding-left: calc(1rem + 13rem + 1.5rem);
  }
}
@media (max-width: 1279.98px) {
  /* Below the split the rail is a toggle above the form, so what it needs is
     height: #content's own 1rem plus the button. The 1rem margin under the
     button is deliberately not counted -- it collapses and does not move the
     form, so booking it would trade one jump for another. */
  .software-content.pg-awaiting-rail > #content {
    padding-top: calc(1rem + var(--pg-tap));
  }
}

/* The button and the rail travel together: the panel hangs off this box, so
   it lands under the button rather than under whatever happens to be the
   nearest positioned ancestor. */
.pg-settings-menu {
  position: relative;
  /* No height here on purpose either, and this one is the trap. The box does
     have to be as tall as the page beside it -- but the flex row above already
     does that by stretching it. height: 100% looks like it says the same thing
     and does the opposite: a declared cross size means the item is no longer
     auto-sized, stretch is skipped, and the box comes out the height of the
     nav. That is what leaves the rail scrolling away with the form. */
}

/* The toggle exists only where the rail becomes a panel; Bootstrap's own
   d-lg-none keeps it off the desktop layout. */
.pg-settings-toggle {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  width: 100%;
  min-height: var(--pg-tap);
  padding: 0 0.75rem;
  border: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  background: var(--pg-tint);
  color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  text-align: left;
}
.pg-settings-toggle-text {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  overflow: hidden;
  white-space: nowrap;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
.pg-settings-toggle-icon {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  color: var(--pg-primary-ink);
}
.pg-settings-toggle-chev {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  font-size: 0.7rem;
  opacity: 0.6;
  transition: transform 0.15s ease;
}
.pg-settings-toggle[aria-expanded="true"] .pg-settings-toggle-chev {
  transform: rotate(180deg);
}

/* Where the grid stacks the two columns the rail stops being a column and
   becomes a panel that drops from under the header. It overlays the form
   rather than pushing it down, so choosing a section does not move the page
   under the finger that is choosing. */
@media (max-width: 1279.98px) {
  .pg-settings-toggle {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--pg-navbar-h) + 0.25rem);
    z-index: 3;
  }
  .pg-settings-nav {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(100% + 0.25rem);
    right: 0;
    left: 0;
    z-index: 3;
    display: none;
    max-height: 60vh;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0.5rem;
    border: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
    border-radius: var(--pg-radius-lg);
    background: var(--pg-bg-body);
    box-shadow: var(--pg-shadow);
  }
  .pg-settings-nav.is-open {
    display: flex;
  }
}
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .pg-settings-toggle-chev {
    transition: none;
  }
}

/* ── Loading curtain and leaving bar ──────────────────────────────────────
 * Printed straight after <body> by pg_preloader_markup().
 *
 * Arriving, the curtain is the page's own background with a bar across the
 * top. It stays transparent for its first 120 ms and fades in over 100 ms,
 * so a screen ready by then shows nothing; the script fades it out again in
 * 100 ms once the document is ready. Only a held curtain (the visual editor,
 * .is-hold) shows the mark in the middle.
 *
 * The failsafe animation is the point of the block: if the inline script
 * never runs — an error above it, a blocked inline script — the curtain
 * would otherwise sit over an unusable panel forever. Ten seconds is long
 * enough that a slow page never sees it and short enough that a broken one
 * is not lost.
 *
 * Leaving, there is no curtain: html.pg-is-leaving shows the thin bar at the
 * top and the progress pointer over the old page until it is gone.
 * ---------------------------------------------------------------------- */
.pg-preloader {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 20000;
    background: var(--pg-bg-body, #fff);
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: visible;
    animation: pg-preloader-arrive .1s ease .12s forwards, pg-preloader-failsafe 0s linear 10s forwards;
}
/* visibility waits for the fade, so the hidden curtain is out of the layer
   tree entirely rather than sitting invisibly on top of every screen. */
.pg-preloader.is-done {
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    transition: opacity .1s ease, visibility 0s linear .1s;
}
/* A done curtain stops its loops rather than running them unseen. */
.pg-preloader.is-done .pg-preloader-bar i,
.pg-preloader.is-done .pg-preloader-dots i {
    animation-play-state: paused;
}

.pg-preloader-bar,
.pg-leave-bar {
    position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; right: 0; height: 3px;
    overflow: hidden; background: rgba(128, 128, 128, .16);
}
.pg-preloader-bar i,
.pg-leave-bar i {
    position: absolute; top: 0; bottom: 0; left: -40%; width: 40%;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, var(--pg-primary, #6366f1), transparent);
    animation: pg-preloader-slide 1.05s cubic-bezier(.4, 0, .2, 1) infinite;
}
@keyframes pg-preloader-slide { to { left: 100%; } }
@keyframes pg-preloader-arrive { to { opacity: 1; } }

/* The leaving bar: display:none keeps its loop stopped until it is wanted. */
.pg-leave-bar {
    position: fixed;
    z-index: 20001;
    display: none;
    pointer-events: none;
}
html.pg-is-leaving .pg-leave-bar { display: block; }
html.pg-is-leaving,
html.pg-is-leaving * { cursor: progress !important; }

/* The mark in the middle: the brand lockup with the dots under it, or on a
   private-label install the dots alone. Only a held curtain draws it, and a
   beat late, so everyday transitions never show a logo. */
.pg-preloader-mark {
    position: absolute; left: 50%; top: 50%; transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    display: none; flex-direction: column; align-items: center; gap: 18px;
    opacity: 0;
}
.pg-preloader.is-hold .pg-preloader-mark {
    display: flex;
    animation: pg-preloader-appear .45s ease .3s forwards;
}
.pg-preloader-logo { display: block; }
.pg-preloader-logo { --pg-logo-h: min(56px, 13vw); }
.pg-preloader-dots {
    display: flex; gap: 7px;
}
.pg-preloader-dots i {
    width: 7px; height: 7px; border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--pg-fg-2, #6c757d);
    animation: pg-preloader-pulse 1.1s ease-in-out infinite;
}
.pg-preloader-dots i:nth-child(2) { animation-delay: .15s; }
.pg-preloader-dots i:nth-child(3) { animation-delay: .3s; }
@keyframes pg-preloader-appear { to { opacity: 1; } }
@keyframes pg-preloader-pulse { 0%, 100% { opacity: .25; transform: translateY(0); } 50% { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(-3px); } }
@keyframes pg-preloader-failsafe { to { opacity: 0; visibility: hidden; pointer-events: none; } }

/* Less motion keeps every delay (a delay is not motion) and drops the fades
   and the sliding loops. */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .pg-preloader { animation-duration: 0s, 0s; }
    .pg-preloader.is-done { transition: none; }
    .pg-preloader-bar i,
    .pg-leave-bar i { animation: none; left: 0; width: 100%; opacity: .5; }
    .pg-preloader.is-hold .pg-preloader-mark { animation-duration: 0s; }
    .pg-preloader-dots i { animation: none; opacity: .6; transform: none; }
}
@media print { .pg-preloader, .pg-leave-bar { display: none !important; } }

/* ══ Admin screen — user permission rows ══════════════════════════════════
   Shared by add_user.php and edit_user.php through includes/user_permissions.php.
   One row per rights group: gate switch, glyph, title, a live summary of what
   is selected, and a button that opens the group's offcanvas panel. Everything
   here is layout and state; the panels themselves are Bootstrap offcanvas. */
.pg-perms {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
}
.pg-perm {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  padding: 0.5rem 1rem;
  border-top: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
}
.pg-perm:first-child {
  border-top: 0;
}
/* The gate is a bare switch: its label is visually-hidden because the title
   beside it already names the row, and a second copy would be read twice. */
.pg-perm-gate-box {
  min-height: 0;
  margin-bottom: 0;
  padding-left: 2.25rem;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
}
.pg-perm-icon {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 1.875rem;
  height: 1.875rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  font-size: 0.9375rem;
  /* Set per row from PHP; the tint is derived so one value carries both. */
  color: var(--pg-perm-color, var(--pg-fg-2));
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-perm-color, currentColor) 16%, transparent);
}
.pg-perm-text {
  min-width: 0;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}
.pg-perm-text b {
  display: block;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  line-height: 1.25;
}
.pg-perm-text span {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 0.125rem;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-perm-summary {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.375rem;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  justify-content: flex-end;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-perm-pill {
  padding: 0.1875rem 0.5rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius-pill);
  border: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-primary-border-subtle);
  background: var(--pg-primary-bg-subtle);
  color: var(--pg-primary-fg-emphasis);
  font-weight: 600;
}
/* A counter with nothing in it is not drawn at all: an empty group already says
   so through the dimmed row, and a row of zeroes reads as broken. */
.pg-perm-pill:empty {
  display: none;
}
.pg-perm-none {
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-perm-open {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
}
/* Off: the row steps back to the secondary text colour instead of an opacity,
   which took the title, description and "Choose" button under 3:1; every
   label stays 4.5:1 in both themes because a new user starts with all rows
   off and picks by reading them. The icon loses its group colour. */
.pg-perm.is-off .pg-perm-icon {
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--pg-fg-2) 12%, transparent);
}
.pg-perm.is-off .pg-perm-text b {
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}

/* The whole block is hidden for the roles that carry every right; the notice
   below takes its place. */
.pg-perm-everything {
  display: flex;
  align-items: flex-start;
  gap: 0.75rem;
}
.pg-perm-everything > i {
  font-size: 1.125rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
}
.pg-perm-everything-list {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 0.375rem;
  margin-top: 0.5rem;
}
.pg-perm-everything-list span {
  padding: 0.125rem 0.5rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius-sm);
  background: var(--pg-tint);
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
}

/* ── The panels ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Wider than Bootstrap's default because a folder tree indents, and a tree that
   wraps is unreadable. Capped at the viewport so a narrow screen still shows
   the page behind it. */
.pg-perm-panel {
  --pg-drawer-width: min(32rem, 100vw - 2rem);
}
.pg-perm-panel .drawer-header {
  align-items: center;
  border-bottom: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
}
.pg-perm-panel .drawer-title {
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-perm-panel .drawer-body {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 1rem;
}
.pg-perm-panel-lead {
  margin: 0;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-perm-panel-lead:empty {
  display: none;
}
.pg-perm-panel .drawer-footer {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
  border-top: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-perm-panel-count {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}
.pg-perm-block-title {
  margin-bottom: 0.375rem;
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  letter-spacing: 0.05em;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
/* The list scrolls inside the panel rather than inside the page - the reason
   the panel exists. max-height is on the card body as before, but now it is the
   only scrolling box on screen, so the wheel cannot land in the wrong one. */
.pg-perm-list {
  margin-bottom: 0;
}
.pg-perm-list .card-body {
  max-height: 22rem;
  overflow: auto;
  padding-top: 0.5rem;
}
.pg-perm-switches {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 0.625rem;
}
.pg-perm-switch :is(.form-field > label) {
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
}
.pg-perm-switch .form-text {
  margin-top: 0.125rem;
  padding-left: 2.5rem;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
}

@media (max-width: 767.98px) {
  /* The summary and the button drop under the title rather than squeezing it;
     the gate and the glyph keep the first line. */
  .pg-perm {
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }
  .pg-perm-text {
    flex: 1 1 60%;
  }
  .pg-perm-summary {
    order: 3;
    flex: 1 1 100%;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    padding-left: 4.75rem;
  }
  .pg-perm-open {
    order: 2;
  }
}

/* ══ Admin screen — user identity header ══════════════════════════════════
   edit_user.php: who this account is, plus the actions that act on the account
   itself rather than on the form (sign in as, reset password, unlock). */
.pg-identity {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.875rem;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
.pg-identity-pic {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 2.875rem;
  height: 2.875rem;
  border-radius: 50%;
  object-fit: cover;
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  font-size: 1.0625rem;
  font-weight: 600;
  color: #fff;
  background: var(--users-color, #7b1fa2);
}
.pg-identity-who {
  min-width: 0;
}
.pg-identity-name {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  font-size: 1rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-identity-meta {
  margin-top: 0.1875rem;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-identity-actions {
  margin-left: auto;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}

/* ══ Admin screen — account side cards ════════════════════════════════════
   edit_user.php's right column: contact, sign-in and sessions, recent activity.
   Rows of read-mostly facts, each with at most one action. */
.pg-fact {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.6875rem;
  padding: 0.5625rem 0;
  border-top: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
}
.pg-fact:first-child {
  border-top: 0;
  padding-top: 0;
}
.pg-fact-icon {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 1.625rem;
  height: 1.625rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  display: grid;
  place-items: center;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  background: var(--pg-tint);
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-fact-text {
  min-width: 0;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}
.pg-fact-text b {
  display: block;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-fact-text span {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 0.125rem;
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-fact-action {
  margin-left: auto;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
}

.pg-contact-card {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
}
.pg-contact-pic {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 3.25rem;
  height: 3.25rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius-lg);
  object-fit: cover;
}
.pg-contact-who {
  min-width: 0;
}
.pg-contact-who b {
  display: block;
  font-size: 0.84375rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-contact-who span {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 0.125rem;
  font-size: 0.71875rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  overflow: hidden;
  text-overflow: ellipsis;
}

.pg-log-line {
  display: flex;
  gap: 0.625rem;
  padding: 0.4375rem 0;
  border-top: var(--pg-border-width) dashed var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  font-size: 0.71875rem;
}
.pg-log-line:first-child {
  border-top: 0;
}
.pg-log-when {
  flex: 0 0 5rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-log-what {
  min-width: 0;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}
.pg-log-ip {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}

/* Sessions inside their panel: device and badges on the first line, the dates
   on the second. A six column table wraps the device name onto three lines at
   this width, which is why this is not a table. */
.pg-session {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
  padding: 0.6875rem 0.875rem;
  border-top: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
}
.pg-session:first-child {
  border-top: 0;
}
.pg-session > i {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  font-size: 1.0625rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-session-text {
  min-width: 0;
  flex: 1 1 auto;
}
.pg-session-name {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.4375rem;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  font-size: 0.78125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-session-meta {
  margin-top: 0.1875rem;
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-session-actions {
  margin-left: auto;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  display: flex;
  gap: 0.375rem;
}

/* Role picker: radio cards instead of a picklist, so the four roles and what
   each one means are readable without opening anything. btn-check hides the
   input; the label is the card. */
.pg-roles {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(11rem, 1fr));
  gap: 0.625rem;
}
.pg-role-card {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 100%;
  padding: 0.5625rem 0.75rem;
  border: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color);
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  background: var(--pg-bg-body);
  cursor: pointer;
}
.pg-role-card b {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.4375rem;
  font-size: 0.8125rem;
  font-weight: 600;
}
.pg-role-card b i {
  font-size: 0.875rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-role-card span {
  display: block;
  margin-top: 0.1875rem;
  padding-left: 1.3125rem;
  font-size: 0.71875rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.btn-check:checked + .pg-role-card {
  border-color: var(--pg-primary);
  background: var(--pg-primary-bg-subtle);
}
.btn-check:checked + .pg-role-card b,
.btn-check:checked + .pg-role-card b i {
  color: var(--pg-primary-fg-emphasis);
}
.btn-check:focus-visible + .pg-role-card {
  outline: var(--pg-focus-ring-width) solid var(--pg-primary-ink);
  outline-offset: var(--pg-focus-ring-offset);
}
.btn-check:disabled + .pg-role-card {
  opacity: 0.6;
  cursor: default;
}

/* While a side panel is open the chat launcher would cover its footer: it is
   fixed at a higher z-index than Bootstrap's offcanvas. */
body.pg-side-panel-open .pg-chat-launcher {
  display: none;
}

/* Rows inside a permission list. Flex rather than Bootstrap's float, so a row
   can carry something after its label - the expiry box below - and so that box
   can take a line of its own by asking for the full basis. */
.pg-perm-list .form-field {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  padding-left: 0;
  margin-bottom: 0.125rem;
}
.pg-perm-list .form-field > :is(.check, .radio) {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  float: none;
  margin: 0;
}
.pg-perm-list .form-field > :is(.form-field > label) {
  flex: 1 1 auto;
  min-width: 0;
}

/* Expiration date on a private-folder grant (get_acl_folder_tree('view')).
   Closed it is a button at the end of the folder's row and costs no height;
   open it takes the next line, because the label before it is as wide as the
   folder name plus its indentation and an inline field would wrap into the
   checkbox column. */
.pg-acl-expiry {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
}
.pg-acl-expiry.is-open {
  flex: 1 1 100%;
  gap: 0.5rem;
  margin: 0.125rem 0 0.375rem 1.75rem;
}
.pg-acl-expiry-add {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.3125rem;
  padding: 0.125rem 0.5rem;
  border: var(--pg-border-width) dashed var(--pg-border-color);
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius-pill);
  background: transparent;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  line-height: 1.4;
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-acl-expiry-add:hover,
.pg-acl-expiry-add:focus-visible {
  border-color: var(--pg-primary);
  color: var(--pg-primary-fg-emphasis);
}
.pg-acl-expiry.is-open .pg-acl-expiry-add {
  display: none;
}
.pg-acl-expiry-field {
  display: none;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.5rem;
}
.pg-acl-expiry.is-open .pg-acl-expiry-field {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}
/* The drawer's calendar is drawn open (pg_fields.js): on a line of its own
   under the field and its clear button. */
.pg-acl-expiry-field > .pg-date-inline {
  flex: 1 1 100%;
  order: 1;
  margin-top: 0;
}
.pg-acl-expiry-label {
  white-space: nowrap;
}
.pg-acl-expiry-input.form-control {
  flex: 0 0 auto;
  width: 8rem;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
}
.pg-acl-expiry-clear {
  border: 0;
  background: transparent;
  padding: 0.125rem 0.25rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  line-height: 1;
}
.pg-acl-expiry-clear:hover,
.pg-acl-expiry-clear:focus-visible {
  color: var(--pg-danger-fg-emphasis);
}

/* How much of a list is selected, beside its "select all" switch. The switch
   itself carries the third state (indeterminate) that Bootstrap already draws. */
.pg-perm-list .card-header {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 0.75rem;
}
.pg-perm-list-count {
  margin-left: auto;
  font-size: 0.6875rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  white-space: nowrap;
}


/* ══ Site Settings: the hub and the category screens ══════════════════════
   The settings were one screen with sixteen cards until 2026-09-12; they are
   now a hub of seven tiles and a screen per category. What is here is the tile,
   the chip and the save bar -- the grid, the cards and every control inside
   them are Bootstrap's. */

.pg-hub-search { max-width: 34rem; }
.pg-hub-search .form-control,
.pg-hub-search .input-group-text { padding-top: .55rem; padding-bottom: .55rem; }

/* A tile is a card you press. The whole head is the link, so the icon, the
   name and the count are one target rather than three. */
.pg-tile-head {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .75rem;
  margin-bottom: .5rem;
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}
.pg-tile-icon {
  display: flex;
  flex: 0 0 2.5rem;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  width: 2.5rem;
  height: 2.5rem;
  border-radius: var(--pg-radius);
  background: var(--pg-tint);
  color: var(--pg-primary);
  font-size: 1.1rem;
}
.pg-tile-title { display: flex; flex-direction: column; min-width: 0; }
.pg-tile-name { font-size: 1.05rem; font-weight: 600; line-height: 1.2; }
.pg-tile-count { color: var(--pg-fg-2); font-size: .75rem; }
.pg-tile-go { margin-left: auto; color: var(--pg-fg-2); font-size: .8rem; }
.pg-tile-head:hover .pg-tile-name { color: var(--pg-primary); }
.pg-tile-head:hover .pg-tile-go { color: var(--pg-primary); }

.pg-tile-desc {
  margin-bottom: .75rem;
  color: var(--pg-fg-2);
  font-size: .8125rem;
}

/* The status line closes the card. Tiles are sized by their own content, so
   there is nothing to push it against. */
.pg-tile-status {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  gap: .4rem;
  padding-top: .75rem;
  border-top: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  font-size: .75rem;
}
.pg-tile-status .bi-circle-fill { font-size: .5rem; }

/* Chips: a section of a category, on the tile and again in the category
   toolbar. Small enough that a list of six reads as one control. */
.pg-chips { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .35rem; }
/* Bootstrap's .chip in the toolbar's size and colours. The chip field's
   rule (.pg-chips > .chip) lets a chip shrink and cut its text; a strip
   scrolls instead, so its chips keep their width. */
.pg-toolbar .pg-chips > .chip {
  --bs-chip-height: auto;
  --bs-chip-padding-x: .55rem;
  --bs-chip-border-radius: 2rem;
  --bs-chip-font-size: .75rem;
  --bs-chip-line-height: 1.4;
  --bs-chip-color: var(--pg-fg-body);
  --bs-chip-bg: var(--pg-bg-body);
  --bs-chip-border-color: var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  --bs-chip-selected-color: var(--pg-primary);
  --bs-chip-selected-bg: var(--pg-tint-strong);
  --bs-chip-selected-border-color: var(--pg-primary);
  min-width: auto;
  overflow: visible;
  padding-block: .2rem;
}
.pg-toolbar .pg-chips > .chip:not(.active):is(:hover, :focus-visible) {
  --bs-chip-color: var(--pg-primary);
  --bs-chip-bg: var(--pg-tint);
  --bs-chip-border-color: var(--pg-primary);
}
.pg-toolbar .pg-chips > .chip.active {
  font-weight: 600;
  opacity: 1;
}
/* Bootstrap takes the outline off; keyboard focus keeps the browser's ring */
.pg-toolbar .pg-chips > .chip:focus-visible {
  outline-style: auto;
}

/* The chip strip scrolls inside the toolbar rather than wrapping it onto a
   second line: the bar keeps one height and the search stays where it was. */
.pg-toolbar .pg-chips {
  flex-wrap: nowrap;
  overflow-x: auto;
  scrollbar-width: thin;
  /* Room for a chip's focus ring inside the scrolling (so clipping) strip;
     the block margin keeps the bar's height. */
  padding: 4px;
  margin-block: -4px;
}

/* ── The cards of a category screen ──────────────────────────────────────
   One card per row, the full width of the screen.
 *
   Two other layouts were tried and both failed for the same reason: these
   cards are dense forms whose fields are laid out on Bootstrap's grid. Put
   two side by side and the row is as tall as the taller one, with the short
   card sitting in a pool of empty space. Put them in narrow masonry columns
   and every field inside stacks, so the cards run on down the page -- which
   is the one thing a settings screen must not do.
 *
   Full width lets each card use the grid it was written with: three fields
   across instead of three fields down. The screen is then as short as its
   content allows, which is what makes it readable.
 *
   The gap is the container's, on the page (#pg_settings_sections) and in the
   dialog (#pg_settings_modal_pane) alike. A card's inline script or a hidden
   Connect form can stand between two cards, and a top margin on a card that
   directly follows another card then left the next card touching the one
   above. An element that draws nothing, a card the filter hides
   included, takes no part in a flex gap. */
.pg-settings-body {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 16px;
}
/* A settings pane's text reads at the look's base size (14px): what sets no
   size of its own (a list row's title, a connection's name, a table cell)
   took the body's 16px and stood larger than the row holding it. A note or
   warning box reads at the help size. */
.pg-settings-body,
#pg_settings_sections {
  font-size: var(--pg-text-base);
}
.pg-settings-body .alert,
#pg_settings_sections .alert {
  font-size: var(--pg-text-2);
}

/* Bootstrap's columns answer to the window, not to the box they are in. That
   is right at full width and wrong on a phone, where a card is the whole of a
   narrow screen: a field marked col-xxl-4 would still take a third of it.
   The card is made a container and its fields stack while it is narrow. */
.pg-set-card { container-type: inline-size; }

@container (max-width: 34rem) {
  .pg-set-card .row > [class*="col-"] {
    flex: 0 0 100%;
    max-width: 100%;
  }
}

/* ── how wide a field is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
   Two questions, and they are answered separately.

   How many to a ROW: one, or at most two. Three abreast reads as squeezed
   however wide the card is -- the labels start wrapping, the values line up
   with nothing, and there is no column for the eye to follow. Six abreast is
   what the Merchant Center block used to do. So a cell is either the whole
   row or half of it, and that is the entire grid.

   How wide the CONTROL is: the class says what the field HOLDS and the
   control takes only the room that needs. A three-character limit stretched
   across half a card reads as an empty box, and the operator starts wondering
   what else is supposed to go in it.

     pg-f-xs   a number or code of a few characters, a time, a percentage
     pg-f-sm   a short value: a postcode, a phone number, an id, a quantity
     pg-f-md   a name, a city, an amount, a picklist
     pg-f-lg   an e-mail address, a hostname, a key -- a row of its own
     (no class) a URL, a sentence, a textarea, a code block, a tag field

   Keyed to the width of the CARD and not of the window, which is the whole
   point of the container query: the same card is drawn about 300px wide on a
   phone, 900 inside the settings dialog's pane and 1400 on the settings page,
   so a viewport breakpoint cannot say "half of this card". A field always has
   the whole row while the card is narrow; nothing here needs a phone rule. */
.pg-set-card .row > .pg-f-xs,
.pg-set-card .row > .pg-f-sm,
.pg-set-card .row > .pg-f-md,
.pg-set-card .row > .pg-f-lg {
  flex: 0 0 100%;
  max-width: 100%;
}

@container (min-width: 34rem) {
  .pg-set-card .row > .pg-f-xs,
  .pg-set-card .row > .pg-f-sm,
  .pg-set-card .row > .pg-f-md {
    flex-basis: 50%;
    max-width: 50%;
  }
}

/* ── one setting to a line ────────────────────────────────────────────────
   A settings card reads as a list of decisions, so it is drawn as one: the
   name of the setting and its explanation on the left, the control that sets
   it on the right, and a rule between one decision and the next. Two fields
   abreast made the eye jump; a control floating under its own label made the
   card a wall.

   Applied from here rather than by rewriting the cards, and opted into by
   SHAPE: a cell that holds a label and one ordinary control, or a cell that
   holds one switch. Everything else -- a textarea, a code field, a tag field,
   a table, a group of buttons, a nested row -- is left exactly as it was,
   because those want the whole width and always did.

   :has() is the test, so a browser without it keeps the old stacked layout
   rather than half of this one. That is the right way to be missing a feature
   in a stylesheet: the screen still works, it is just plainer. */
@supports selector(:has(*)) {

  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-label):has(> input.form-control:not(.tagin), > :is(select.form-control), > .input-group, > .form-adorn, > .form-range),
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-field > :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)) {
    /* The rule and the text start at the same place, which is the card's own
       content edge. A .row carries a negative horizontal margin and gives each
       cell the matching padding back, so a border drawn on the cell runs half a
       gutter WIDER than the words inside it -- the line reaches past the text at
       both ends and the card looks out of true. The padding is handed to the
       margin instead: same position for the text, and the line now begins and
       ends exactly where the text does. */
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: calc(100% - var(--bs-gutter-x));
    max-width: calc(100% - var(--bs-gutter-x));
    margin-top: 0;
    margin-inline: calc(var(--bs-gutter-x) * .5);
    padding-inline: 0;
    padding-block: 0 var(--pg-set-row-y);
  }

  /* One rhythm, not two. Bootstrap spaces the lines of a .row with a gutter:
     a top margin on every cell, cancelled on the first line by an equal
     negative margin on the row itself. A converted cell spaces itself with its
     own padding and gives that margin up, which leaves the row's negative half
     uncompensated -- measured, the card's 1rem of body padding vanished and
     the first setting sat flush against the header in every one of the eight
     panes. Mixing the two also gave one card two different gaps depending on
     which kind of cell happened to come next.

     So the gutter is handed over whole: the row keeps none of it and every
     cell, converted or not, is spaced by the same padding underneath. The row
     then takes that last cell's padding back off the bottom, so what shows at
     both edges of the card is the card's own padding, which is what it was
     always for. (A hidden cell renders no padding, so the row ends at the last
     VISIBLE cell either way.)

     Written with the gutter variable rather than a :has() test for the rows
     that need it: :has() may not be nested inside :has(), and "a row holding a
     converted cell" needs exactly that -- the selector parses as invalid and
     the rule is dropped without a word. */
  .pg-set-card .row {
    --bs-gutter-y: 0;
  }
  .pg-set-card .row > * {
    padding-bottom: var(--pg-set-row-y);
  }
  .pg-set-card .card-body > .row {
    margin-bottom: calc(var(--pg-set-row-y) * -1);
  }

  /* The rule belongs BETWEEN two decisions, so it is drawn on the second one
     and there is no such thing as a trailing line. Drawn on the first would
     need "the last one visible" and a stylesheet cannot ask that: a switch that
     opens a block keeps that block in the page while it is shut, so the last
     row on screen is hardly ever the last child. A card with one setting in it
     -- Sign in with Google, when it is off -- then ended with a rule under the
     only thing in it and a gap under that. A cell that is not a field but is
     a decision of its own (IndexNow's connection under SEO) is marked
     .pg-set-ruled. */
  .pg-set-card .row > * + [class]:has(> .form-label):has(> input.form-control:not(.tagin), > :is(select.form-control), > .input-group, > .form-adorn, > .form-range),
  .pg-set-card .row > * + [class]:has(> .form-field > :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)),
  .pg-set-card .row > * + .pg-set-ruled {
    padding-top: var(--pg-set-row-y);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  }

  /* A group heading inside a card (a cell that starts with an <h6>) starts a new
     group, so the line goes above it, with room, and not between it and the
     group's first setting: drawn there, the heading sat flush under the
     previous setting's description and read as part of it. The row right
     after a heading repeats the divider's predicate to take the line back
     off; a plain selector could not beat it. */
  .pg-set-card .row > * + [class]:has(> h6:first-child) {
    /* Padding handed to the margin as on a converted cell, so the line
       starts and ends where the text does. */
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: calc(100% - var(--bs-gutter-x));
    max-width: calc(100% - var(--bs-gutter-x));
    margin-inline: calc(var(--bs-gutter-x) * .5);
    padding-inline: 0;
    margin-top: .5rem;
    padding-top: 1.1rem;
    border-top: var(--pg-border-width) solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  }
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> h6:first-child) + [class]:has(> .form-label):has(> input.form-control:not(.tagin), > :is(select.form-control), > .input-group, > .form-adorn, > .form-range),
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> h6:first-child) + [class]:has(> .form-field > :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)) {
    padding-top: .25rem;
    border-top: 0;
  }

  /* Settings a switch shows or hides together sit in a cell of their own
     (one element to hide), so the first of them is the first child of a
     nested row and the rule above was never drawn on it: under Alerts,
     "Send alerts by e-mail" read as part of the master switch's
     description. A cell holding only a nested row hands the line on.
     Written with .row:only-child, never as :has(> .row:only-child) on the
     cell: a :has() that is not the subject of its selector makes Chromium
     restyle every [class] element under the body after any DOM change,
     which in the visual designer cost a style pass over thousands of
     elements on every click. */
  .pg-set-card .row > * + [class]:not(.collapse) > .row:only-child > [class]:first-child:has(> .form-label):has(> input.form-control:not(.tagin), > :is(select.form-control), > .input-group, > .form-adorn, > .form-range),
  .pg-set-card .row > * + [class]:not(.collapse) > .row:only-child > [class]:first-child:has(> .form-field > :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)) {
    padding-top: var(--pg-set-row-y);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--pg-border-color-translucent);
  }

  /* The two columns. The control column is given a width rather than left to
     its content: a .form-control is width:100%, and in a content-sized column
     that is a question with no answer -- the field would end up as wide as
     whatever happened to be typed in it. The pg-f-* caps narrow it from there,
     so a two-digit limit still gets a two-digit box.

     The name and the control share the FIRST row and the explanation goes
     under both. The control used to span both rows so that it would centre
     against the name and its explanation together, and that is what put every
     row out of true: a spanning item hands its height to the tracks it spans,
     so an empty second row still took a share of the control's height and
     lifted the name out of the middle. Measured across the eight panes: every
     plain field 7px out, every input group 19px, the icon picker 28px -- and
     the amount changed with the control, which is what made the card look
     crooked rather than merely offset. Sharing one row makes that row as tall
     as whichever of the two is taller and centres both in it, at any height. */
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-label):has(> input.form-control:not(.tagin), > :is(select.form-control), > .input-group, > .form-adorn, > .form-range),
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-field > :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)) > .form-field {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(7rem, 19rem);
    grid-template-areas:
      "pgname pgctrl"
      "pgnote pgnote";
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 1.5rem;
    padding-left: 0;
    min-height: 0;
  }

  /* A select shows its chosen value whole. The 19rem column cut every option
     longer than about 250px (a time zone, a mail connection, a folder or an
     offer by name) whatever width class the cell had, so a select's column
     is at least 19rem and otherwise as wide as its longest option: the
     intrinsic width of a select is that option's. The pg-f-* cap on the
     select still holds it (a max-width clamps the column's measure too), a
     col-12 cell has none, and the name keeps 12rem. Stated before the phone
     rule below, which then wins with the same weight. */
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-label):has(> select.form-control:not([multiple])) {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(min(12rem, 100%), 1fr) minmax(min(19rem, 100%), max-content);
  }

  /* Anything the two columns were not expecting takes a line of its own under
     them rather than being wedged into a cell it does not belong in. Stated
     before the placements below so that the placements win.

     The predicate sits on the child itself (the child is, or has a sibling
     that is, the label and the control), never on the cell followed by
     `> *`. Chromium answers a rule of the form `X:has(…) > *` by restyling
     the WHOLE document on every element added or removed anywhere on the
     page, whether or not any X exists: on the visual editor (some 18,000
     elements) that was 250-450 ms per redraw. A sibling :has() on the
     subject is restyled only where the siblings change. The weight is the
     old rule's (0,6,1), so the placements below still win. The second
     selector holds a field whose own children include the switch; the third
     a field after such a one in the same cell. A field BEFORE it cannot be
     reached from the child without the slow form; no settings card puts a
     switch field beside another field in one cell. */
  .pg-set-card .row > [class] > :is(.form-label, .form-label ~ *, :has(~ .form-label)):is(input.form-control:not(.tagin), select.form-control, .input-group, .form-adorn, .form-range, :is(input.form-control:not(.tagin), select.form-control, .input-group, .form-adorn, .form-range) ~ *, :has(~ :is(input.form-control:not(.tagin), select.form-control, .input-group, .form-adorn, .form-range))),
  .pg-set-card .row > [class] > .form-field > :is(:is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch), :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch) ~ *, :has(~ :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch))):is(.form-field > *),
  .pg-set-card .row > [class] > .form-field:has(> :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)) ~ .form-field > * {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
  }

  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-label):has(> input.form-control:not(.tagin), > :is(select.form-control), > .input-group, > .form-adorn, > .form-range) > .form-label,
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-field > :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)) > .form-field > :is(.form-field > label) {
    grid-area: pgname;
    margin-bottom: 0;
    font-size: var(--pg-text-base);
    font-weight: var(--pg-weight-strong);
  }

  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-label):has(> input.form-control:not(.tagin), > :is(select.form-control), > .input-group, > .form-adorn, > .form-range) > .form-text,
  .pg-set-card .row > [class]:has(> .form-field > :is(input[type="checkbox"], .switch)) > .form-field > .form-text {
    grid-area: pgnote;
    margin-top: 1px;
  }

  /* The grid has one note area, so a second description (a warning drawn
     only on some sites, a link to the screen that holds the rest) would be
